ole=3D"message" dir=3D"auto"><div><p>Sociologist Judy Wajcman wrote about t=
he concept of how tech is speeding up tasks precisely like this article des=
cribes, however she observed that it has never quite manifested as more fre=
e time for the laborer.</p><p>Id put it this way, setting aside for a momen=
t the concepts of coding artisans or generally labor as fulfillment for peo=
ple, hasn't there always been enough people to cover the labor requirements=
 of any sector of work? The only issue that has caused employment scarcity =
is company preference for cheaper labor, such is the underlying cause to im=
migration crises.</p><p> If instead of a black box it was a island full of =
people that we transmitted our prompts to and received code back from then =
it would be clear for what this tech is, outsourcing labor because its chea=
per. But, instead of an unknown foreign people, its the unknown countless p=
eople who have coded in the past and have had their work scraped and averag=
ed into the black box.</p><p>What happens when a new coding language is req=
uired? How will we debug it the code it creates? How we can ensure the code=
 it does make is safe and efficient to run? All questions only answerable u=
sing the skills this tech hopes to replace.</p><p>Wajcman also talks on how=
 tech imprints the values of the people who create it, to me AI bares the i=
mprint of people so rich that their only goal is to further stamp out the p=
ower of labor regardless of long-term sustainability and people like Antire=
z who naively believe their campaign will end in a socialist dream.</p></di=
