normal-technology">"normal" technology</a>   , and we can look to the histo=
ry of technology for guidance about what is happening now with generative A=
I.</p><p>I'm not disputing what Claude Code can do--I've experimented enoug=
h to understand, and have been amazed as well. However we cannot sit on our=
 hands and expect leaders will magically appear for us to vote for, who wil=
l ensure the technology isn't centralized, massively inefficient, or depend=
ent on low paid RLHF laborers, as it replaces people's jobs of all kinds. I=
t takes work and organizing to make those people appear. Those people are u=
s.</p><p>We must take a step back from the otherworldly glow of our ClaudeC=
ode terminal sessions as they rap out line after line of code on demand for=
 our latest project idea, and think and talk about the social, political an=
d economic context that this is happening in--and act accordingly. Suggesti=
ng that resisting these genAI coding tools isn't a valid position for a sof=
tware developer to take is ... not a helpful message. It's almost like sugg=
esting that we don't need people talking about how effective LLMs are at pe=
rforming some tasks.</p><p>PS. We also need more science fiction, to better=
 understand our world, and the place of technology in it. I do hope those i=
n power spend a bit more time digesting the plot when it's set in a dystopi=
a...</p></div></div><span class=3D"post-media"><ul data-role=3D"post-media-=
