-message-6822355968" class=3D"post-message " data-role=3D"message" dir=3D"a=
uto"><div><p>If I were an undergrad, I would code the hardest projects I ca=
n think of by myself with no help from an AI, with more and more difficult =
challenges to my own mind, because you need that mental exercise. Undergrad=
 is the exact right time to improve your brain into a sharp instrument. Whe=
n you can look at any large problem and know how you would code it yourself=
, then you may be able to use AI as a tool to boost your efficiency. But fi=
rst you need to know not only what you want a piece of software to do (the =
output you wish to see). You need to know how you would approach the proble=
m (the method you would use to create what you wish to see). Spend your tim=
e making code from the bottom up, thinking about the logic from the most ba=
sic principles. AI is a force-multiplier of your skill, but since it multip=
lies everyone else's skill too, you will be at an even greater disadvantage=
 unless you can explain why things should be done a certain way. AI does no=
t provide those answers, it tends to just reinforce the biases of whoever u=
ses it. You still need to have very good skills to compete. Focus on buildi=
ng those skills without the crutch of AI, and then you will be ahead of eve=
ryone who used it as a crutch in undergrad.</p><p>Write a poker hand parser=
 from scratch in C. Make it as short and fast as possible. Don't look at an=
y other poker parsers, don't ask AI, just use your knowledge of poker hands=
.</p><p>Write a 2D game engine from scratch in Javascript. Don't look at ot=
her game engines.</p><p>Write a demo in 16k. Write a clone of Pacman. Write=
 a screensaver. Write a secure login system with sessions. Write a socket s=
erver. You may ask questions, but don't look at anyone else's work and don'=
t copy or paste anything.</p><p>Then you can approach AI and hope to know i=
f it is writing code that is up to your standards - or the standards of who=
ever you are working for. Then you can use it as a tool and have enough con=
fidence and mastery to decide whether or not to use its output.</p></div></=
