o"><div><p>I don=E2=80=99t think physical jobs are safe either. In the next=
 five years, robotics will probably make big changes across many industries=
.</p><p>But regarding what you said: from my personal experience as a custo=
mer of many different applications and products=E2=80=94lots of B2B and B2C=
 apps=E2=80=94I can say with confidence that a lot of software built by sma=
ll companies is pretty bad. The big problem is getting software truly =E2=
=80=9Cclient-ready,=E2=80=9D because in many cases the application is 90% b=
uilt, but the last 10% takes as much time as the first 90%. All that polish=
ing, refinement, and iteration=E2=80=94changing the same feature 10 times u=
ntil it=E2=80=99s perfectly dialed in=E2=80=94takes a huge amount of effort=
, and small companies usually don=E2=80=99t have the money for that.</p><p>=
As a result, you end up with a million small tools that are basically unusa=
ble. They work for a small subset of customers who need one specific workfl=
ow that=E2=80=99s been tuned for them, but the companies struggle to push b=
eyond that. Polishing doesn=E2=80=99t =E2=80=9Cadd features,=E2=80=9D it ma=
kes the product usable=E2=80=94and that=E2=80=99s hard to market.</p><p>=E2=
=80=9CAlso, we can already see LLMs beginning to code a SaaS or an app on d=
emand. So who is going to invest in someone's work on a project that may ta=
ke $100k to build a MVP app, when anyone's uncle can ask Claude to make it?=
=E2=80=9D</p><p>No one will give someone $100k to build an MVP=E2=80=94but =
that same person might give $10k to 10 different companies to build MVPs, a=
nd one of them will be better than the others. And companies can build soft=
ware for $10k instead of $100k if the software-building process becomes 10=
=C3=97 more efficient.</p><p>But we=E2=80=99ll see :) It will happen whatev=
er we think about it=E2=80=94this is just small talk about inevitable chang=
es.</p></div></div><span class=3D"post-media"><ul data-role=3D"post-media-l=
