2797356" class=3D"post-message " data-role=3D"message" dir=3D"auto"><div><p=
>While your perspective as a pioneer of the "human touch" in software is de=
eply respected, there are several points where your optimism might be overl=
ooking the structural risks of this new era:</p><p>1. The "Understanding" I=
llusion (The Depth Gap) You suggest that writing code is no longer sensible=
 and that understanding "what to do" is the new priority. However, in syste=
ms programming, the how is often the what. When we delegate 700 lines of C =
to an LLM, we bypass the "productive frustration" that builds deep intuitio=
n for memory safety, cache locality, and race conditions. We risk creating =
a generation of "Architects on Sand"=E2=80=94people who can design systems =
but lack the visceral understanding required to fix them when the AI makes =
a high-level, statistically probable, but technically fatal error.</p><p>2.=
 The Innovation Plateau LLMs are, by definition, engines of the "average." =
They predict the next token based on the consensus of existing human knowle=
dge. If the entire industry moves toward prompting instead of manual tinker=
ing, we risk an "Innovation Plateau." True breakthroughs=E2=80=94like the u=
nique architecture of Redis itself=E2=80=94often come from a human deviatin=
g from the "sensible" path. If we all use the same oracle, our software wil=
l become a homogenous soup of "good enough," but rarely "revolutionary."</p=
><p>3. The Myth of AI Democratization Comparing AI to the Open Source movem=
ent of the 90s is tempting but potentially flawed. Open Source required onl=
y a cheap PC and time. Frontier AI requires billions in compute and central=
ized data silos. While "open" models exist today, the gap between the "Mode=
l Elite" and the rest of us is widening. We aren't just using tools; we are=
 becoming dependent on proprietary black boxes owned by a few entities=E2=
=80=94the literal opposite of the Open Source philosophy.</p><p>4. The Main=
tenance Debt Speed is not free. When we generate code in minutes that used =
to take weeks, we are accumulating "Technical Debt at Scale." The person wh=
o didn't write the code is always less equipped to maintain it five years l=
ater when the original prompt is lost and the AI's logic turns out to have =
an edge-case flaw.</p></div></div><span class=3D"post-media"><ul data-role=
